---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_ii1_258",
  "series": "Band II 1 (1663 - 1685)",
  "seq": 258,
  "text": "<p> [[801.1]] <b>227b. ROBERT HOOKE AN THEODOR HAAK FÜR LEIBNIZ</p>\n<p> [[801.2]] [London, 22. Juli 1680.] [223a.228a.]</b></p>\n<p> [[801.17]] S^#'r </p>\n<p> [[801.18]] I am extreamly pleasd to finde by yo^#'r letter that D^#'r Leibniz (the acutnesse of whose\n[[801.19]] invention and solidity of Judgment I very much admire and hono^#'r) has the same apprehensions\n[[801.20]] w^#'t^#'h me concerning the usefullnesse of A philosophicall language and character. An attempt at\n[[801.21]] w^#'c^#'h the late learned Bishop D^#'r Wilkins published in his book called the <i>Reall Character</i> etc. For\n[[801.22]] though that excellent person has not carryed it soe farr and to the height it is possible to be\n[[801.23]] improved for that end, yet what he did was not w^#'t^#'hout a prospect that way, and whilst he was\n[[801.24]] composing of it we had many discourses to that purpose (for I had the happynesse to be w^#'t^#'h him\n[[801.25]] a great part of the time that he was compiling it and have had many debates w^#'t^#'h him of that\n[[801.26]] particular) though I never before I saw your letter heard in the least that it was the opinion of D^#'r\n[[801.27]] Leibnitz. And though I could not Prevaile w^#'t^#'h that Person to modell it according to some Ideas\n[[801.28]] of it which I then conceived yet since his Death I have spent some thoughts upon it and have\n[[801.29]] formed an Idea of it which will shorten it at least half in half, and make it applicable and usefull\n[[801.30]] not only for common Discourse and keeping Correspondence but for the strict and philosophicall\n[[801.31]] way of Reasoning and Inquiry. Nor am I ignorant that the same method of Ratiocination\n[[802.1]] and Demonstration which is made use of in Geometry and for finding out the affections and\n[[802.2]] proprietys of quantity may also be applyed to the search after the proprietys affections and\n[[802.3]] causes of any other subject where we can arrive to a certain knowledge of the principles of it,\n[[802.4]] And even in the inquisition and manifestation of those principles the same method and art will\n[[802.5]] assist us noe lesse then Algebra does Geometry or Arithmetick nor doe I doubt but that in\n[[802.6]] naturall philosophy (for instance) the true proprietys of bodys the power of causes or motions,\n[[802.7]] the effects resulting from conjunctions and the conjunctions and causes from the results may as\n[[802.8]] certainly and demonstrably be evidenced from either of them Given as if both were seen\n[[802.9]] together, and all the Intermediate time of operating. For the art of Reasoning both according to\n[[802.10]] the Analytick and Synthetick method is not to be confined to the speculation of quantity,\n[[802.11]] though that indeed be very extensive and comprise a very great part of man's Speculation, but\n[[802.12]] is as boundlesse as the mind and Soule of man. Nor has this been unseen by severall Authours\n[[802.13]] some whereof have made some essays towards it though very short of what may yet be done, by\n[[802.14]] some that shall more thoroughly consider and examine it. Our learned Lord Bacon may be\n[[802.15]] reconned among one of the best of that kind, And though he had the Infelicity to have some\n[[802.16]] prejudice for mathematicall learning, yet the Extraordinary strength of his naturall and acquired\n[[802.17]] parts carried him on Almost to a miracle in all the efforts of his undertakings. Severall excellent\n[[802.18]] hints to this purpose may be found scatterd here and there in his <i>Novum Organum</i> and some\n[[802.19]] other peices of his writings, But they are but Hints and doe yet need very much of thought and\n[[802.20]] Invention to Produce them into a compleat Art and method. I question not but D^#'r Leibnitz has\n[[802.21]] had very excellent thoughts to this Purpose. And I should be very glad to understand how farr\n[[802.22]] he hath carryed it toward perfection. [ ... ] </p>\n",
  "number": "227b.",
  "title": "ROBERT HOOKE AN THEODOR HAAK FÜR LEIBNIZ",
  "date": "[London, 22. Juli 1680.] [223a.228a.]"
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:227b. ROBERT HOOKE AN THEODOR HAAK FÜR LEIBNIZ [London, 22. Juli 1680.] [223a.228a.] S^#'r I am extreamly pleasd to finde by yo^#'r letter that D^#'r Leibniz (the acutnesse of whose invention and solidity of Judgment I very much admire and hono^#'r) has the same apprehensions w^#'t^#'h me concerning...">
      <div class="text-content">
        {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
          paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

